<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Géza I of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1040–<a href="/years/1077">1077</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Géza I was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1074 to 1077, a son of King Béla I. As holder of the ducal third of the kingdom, he and his brother Ladislaus fought their cousin King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and defeated him at Mogyoród in 1074. His short reign is remembered for the Byzantine crown sent by Emperor Michael VII, which forms the lower part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geza-i-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Géza I of Hungary" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9za_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Géza was the eldest son of the future King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-i-of-hungary">Béla I of Hungary</a> and a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He was born in about 1040, in Poland according to the Illuminated Chronicle, and his baptismal name was Magnus. In about 1048 his father returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and received one third of the kingdom as a duchy from his brother, King Andrew I. Béla became king on 6 December 1060 and died on 11 September 1063, as German troops entered Hungary to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>.</p><p>Géza offered to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s rule in return for his father's duchy. Solomon refused, and Géza and his brothers went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. They returned with Polish reinforcements, and a treaty signed at Győr on 20 January 1064 gave them the duchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> half-denars were minted for Géza as DUX MAGNUS.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Géza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> worked closely until a quarrel at the siege of the Byzantine fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1071. Solomon's adviser Count Vid pushed the king towards war, and the royal army routed Géza at Kemej on 26 February 1074. Géza won the decisive battle of Mogyoród on 14 March 1074 with his brother Ladislaus and Duke Otto I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, who brought Czech reinforcements. Solomon withdrew to Moson, and Géza took almost the whole kingdom. He was not crowned at once because the royal jewels remained with Solomon. In mid-1074 the German monarch Henry IV led an expedition as far as Vác, but Géza used scorched earth tactics and bribed German commanders to persuade him to retreat.</p><p>Géza approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a> for recognition, but the pope tried to win acknowledgement of papal suzerainty and Géza did not obey. He asked Emperor Michael VII Doukas for a crown instead. The emperor sent a gold and enamel diadem inscribed Géza, the faithful king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, which by the end of the 12th century formed the lower part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>. Géza was crowned with it in early 1075.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> still held Moson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, and royal troops under Ladislaus failed to take Pressburg in 1076. According to the Illuminated Chronicle, Géza considered giving up the crown to Solomon from the end of that year and opened peace talks, which came to nothing. He died on 25 April 1077 and was buried in the cathedral of Vác, which he had built in honour of the Holy Virgin. His sons were children, so his brother Ladislaus succeeded him. He had married twice, first Sophia and then Synadene, a niece of Nikephoros Botaneiates; his sons Coloman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia">Álmos</a> were born around 1070.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Géza I of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The arrangement made the duke heir presumptive, but it repeatedly led to war: Béla took the throne from Andrew in 1060, and <b>Géza</b> and Ladislaus fought King Solomon in the 1070s.</blockquote><a href="/state/tercia-pars-regni">Tercia pars regni</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Kingdom of Poland. Died in Vác, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.78,19.13&amp;year=1064" aria-label="Vác on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4996 490 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Géza I of Hungary"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5446 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5446 760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.78,19.13&amp;year=1064">What was here in 1064? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
